WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.750
One of my assistants that
traveled with me, he said,

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.030
after all these years, this
still gets to you, doesn't it?

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.270
I said, man, we putting
a man's body in there

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.490
next to his mother's grave.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.370
Yeah, it gets to me.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.540
And I said, let me tell
you something, young man.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.770
The minute it doesn't
matter to you any more

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.400
you need to leave the movement.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.980
It matters to me.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.259
Every one of these deaths.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.100
Hello and welcome.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.120
If you want a long view of the battle

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.110
for racial justice and equality,

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.150
there are few better
witnesses than my guest today.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.300
He went from pounding
the streets of America

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.690
demanding change,

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.450
to having a direct line
to the White House.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.070
Attracting strong praise
and sharp criticism

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.780
for his style and tactics along the way,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.420
he is the civil rights veteran,

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.220
the Reverend Al Sharpton,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.340
who has been on the frontline

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.090
of many of America's most
notorious police brutality cases,

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.690
including caring for George Floyd's family

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.140
as Black Lives Matter protests
erupted around the world.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.290
Soul legend James Brown
was a father figure to him.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:15.650
And now aged 67

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.880
with a weekly TV and daily
radio show to his name,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.600
he shows no signs of slowing down.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.740
He now has his latest book
out, "Righteous Troublemakers,"

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.450
in which he tells the stories

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.690
of those who have
protest thrown upon them.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.950
Reverend Al, thank you very
much for being with me.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.690
And I thought I'd start by asking,

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.220
is that how you describe yourself,
a righteous troublemaker?

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.140
Well, I do.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.210
I think that it is,

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.550
You know I got the thought
of it from John Lewis,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.740
who was of course the Congressman

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.050
and beloved in the civil rights movement.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.980
He said, make good trouble.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.800
And I think that I've tried to be

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.710
a good troublemaker all my life.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.209
We'll come to your record
and how that's worked

00:01:58.209 --> 00:02:00.620
and some of the tactics and
how things have changed.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.280
But who are you trying to
give voice to in this book?

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.380
I'm trying to give voice to people

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.940
that I feel made tremendous contributions

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.448
but never got the recognition

00:02:12.448 --> 00:02:17.448
and the visibility that I
think that they were merited.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.310
For example,

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.580
most people in the United States

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.910
and in around the Western world

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.970
would not know the name
of Claudette Colvin.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.530
Claudette Colvin,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.230
whose story I write about in this book,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.180
was arrested for sitting
in front of the bus

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.360
in segregated Montgomery, Alabama,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.370
refusing to give up her seat,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.250
nine months before Rosa Parks.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.370
But the local Montgomery black leadership

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:48.940
didn't want to rally around her

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.070
because they said she was too dark skinned

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.360
and she was pregnant and was unmarried.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.550
She didn't have the moral
picture they wanted.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.610
It was her sitting in the front of the bus

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.170
that inspired Rosa Parks.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:05.080
In fact, Fred Gray was the
lawyer for both of them.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:06.900
And I wanted people to understand that,

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.520
so that everyday people would know

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.880
that you don't have to qualify

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.720
to be a troublemaker or a freedom fighter.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.970
Everybody has a contribution.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.500
And these people were just regular people

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.020
that did some things that were amazing.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.750
And yet some of the people

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.710
that you're also talking about in the book

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.790
have protest thrust upon them.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.570
It's not something that
they set out to do,

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.730
and because they find themselves
a miscarriage of justice

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.500
and they find themselves
in the eye of the storm.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.670
How have you found their stories?

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.170
And at times I know you've
personally worked with them

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.760
and tried to get them ready
for something like that.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.440
They're ordinary people

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.920
living just regular lives.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.860
And all of a sudden
they're thrust out here

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:54.123
in the middle of what becomes a maelstrom.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.436
I talk about Philonise Floyd for example.

00:03:58.436 --> 00:04:01.890
Philonise was just a regular
guy in Houston, Texas.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:06.610
Brother George Floyd abused
by police in Minneapolis.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.160
And all of a sudden

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.467
they become a global news story

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:15.650
And a global movement

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.790
But Philonise, unlike me or unlike others,

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.010
didn't choose to be an activist.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:23.660
He's thrust out there.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.900
And that's one of the reasons
why National Action Network

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.030
has been involved in a lot of these cases,

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.640
is you have to remember
when a police officer

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:36.610
is accused of a misdeed or a crime,

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.150
they have the police
unions that back them up

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.760
and provide them with
lawyers and resources

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.120
and if they need a therapist,

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.460
if they need people to
help them with interviews

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.370
or to advise them with media.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.830
Families have none of that.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:53.050
They wake up one morning,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.690
and their brother, their
cousin, their son is dead.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.250
Nobody's there to help them.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.950
That's why National Action
Network provides all of that

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:02.800
because you need an institution

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.490
to go up against the institutions.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:05.667
And we can provide all of that.

00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:08.430
You're talking there
about the organization

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.070
that you head up to, which helps people?

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.430
Exactly.
I mean, you say in the book,

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.780
your job was to build
a firewall of support

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:20.130
around the Floyd family
to guide, to protect them.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.200
I mean, even though what
had happened was horrendous

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.900
and then became the symbol

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.460
of a newly-ignited kind
of global movement,

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.320
do you think that they
could be prepared for it?

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.220
I mean, are they still sort
of coming out of that now?

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.506
I think that they showed tremendous growth

00:05:38.506 --> 00:05:41.340
and tremendous ability,

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.450
but they had to have people

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.290
that would advise and guide them.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.323
So the perception is that,

00:05:47.536 --> 00:05:50.300
oh, with people like Sharpton and others

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.300
run in when there is trouble.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:53.330
No, we'll call them.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.250
The Floyd family called me.

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:59.630
I mean, can you imagine you
are mourning your brother?

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.290
In Philonise's case. Philonise Floyd.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:03.550
Brother was killed.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.060
How he was killed,

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.680
you're watching on television every night,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.410
and how this is killing you.

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:12.480
But there's 10 TV
cameras outside your door

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.800
that wants an immediate response.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.690
And you don't know if you'll say something

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:21.410
that might hurt the case
that you want prosecuted

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:22.750
or just the policemen.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.350
So you need people that will say,

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.040
be careful, don't say this.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:30.440
Don't say something out of
rage that they can use later.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:33.640
All of that comes with
building a movement.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.920
And all of that happens
to people that decide

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.870
I'm going to make trouble
for a system that's unfair

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.090
but I'm going to do it in a righteous way.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.120
And that's why I talk about it.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.420
I got the idea

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.920
because when in the
middle of George Floyd,

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:50.400
since we're talking about it,

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.120
they had asked me to do the eulogy

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:55.120
at both his funeral in Houston

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.310
and his funeral in Minneapolis.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:58.180
And I remember in the middle

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:01.860
of my delivering the eulogy,

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.260
just for whatever reason, it hit me,

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:08.410
because I've always been
an extemporaneous speaker

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.070
because I started
preaching when I was a kid.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:12.220
So I don't use a manuscript.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:15.310
And it hit me, and I said, we
need to march on Washington.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.700
We need to bring the
issue of police reform

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.200
and police misconduct to Washington.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.690
We had over 200,000 people.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.820
And I'm on my way to the steps

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.170
where Dr. King had spoken in 1963

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:31.210
to make the speech.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:34.990
I have the families of
George Floyd with me.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:36.850
Family of Ahmaud Arbery with me.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:39.430
Family of Jacob Blake.
Family of Breonna Taylor.

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:41.540
About 15 victim's families.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.150
We're all walking toward the stage.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.070
And in these throngs of people,

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:48.760
I see an old man that
was jumping up and down

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:51.620
with an object in his hand,
trying to get my attention.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.390
And I say to the security
around me, I said,

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.968
What is that old man trying to tell me?

00:07:55.968 --> 00:07:56.810
And then he says,

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:58.020
Well, Reverend, we need
to keep you moving.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.190
And I said, No, but this old man,

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:00.460
he looks like he's in his eighties.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.710
He's trying to say something. Go get him.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.900
And they went and got
him, brought him to me.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:08.250
And he showed me what he had
in his hand was a button.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.140
And it said, "March on
Washington. Freedom now."

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:15.020
He said, I was here in '63
with Martin Luther King

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.020
and I wanted to be here today with you.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.850
And this is the button I wore in '63.

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:22.860
And that kind of haunted
me. I never forgot that man.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.610
Because he just went back in the crowd.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:29.850
And I said, there are people
that have paid a price,

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.400
that went to the marches,
went to the rallies,

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.490
some went to jail,

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:35.050
some were killed,

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:38.640
that knew they were never
going to be in the newspaper

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:39.870
the next day.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.060
They were not going to be
on the news that night.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.070
They were just righteous troublemakers.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.700
They were there for the cause.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:49.010
Suppose the people like me
called a march and nobody came.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:49.843
These people came.

00:08:49.843 --> 00:08:52.520
200,000 people in the middle of a pandemic

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:55.230
at their own expense, at their own risk.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:57.390
Why don't we that have the limelight

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.200
put a limelight on some of them.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.510
And that's where the
book's concept came from.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:02.560
On that point though,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.910
I was wondering when you get that call,

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.540
you yourself because you have
been doing this for so long,

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.530
faith is obviously a
huge part of your life.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:13.640
How do you deal with that?

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:17.540
Because you're still seeing
people losing their lives.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:21.070
You're still seeing these
police brutality cases.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:26.070
I think if I didn't have a
deep spiritual life and faith,

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:27.750
it would overtake me

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:32.750
because it hurts to
stand over these funerals

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.160
and look at these bodies
over and over again.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.310
I've done since George Floyd

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:41.840
maybe 10 funerals of victims since then.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.600
There's no way that would not wear on you

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:47.320
unless you had a faith to believe

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:50.020
that this is the work
that you were born to do.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:51.650
I'm watching families.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:53.190
I'm trying to comfort them

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.440
and at the same time asking myself,

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.730
God, how long will this last?

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:00.080
How many times do we
have to go through this

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:01.700
before the laws are changed?

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:05.380
So if I were to tell
you it didn't get to me,

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.440
I would be lying.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:09.270
I tell you, and I wrote
about this in the book,

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.430
after George Floyd's funeral,

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.220
the last one in Houston,

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:16.740
and we had the long motorcade to bury him

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.310
next to his mother.

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:21.010
And I remember I did the prayer.

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:23.060
I did the committal of the body.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.380
And then I hugged the family

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.730
and I left to head to the
airport to head back to New York.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:32.270
And I had my head down
and a little teary-eyed.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:35.100
And one of my assistants that
traveled with me, he said,

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:38.410
after all these years, this
still gets to you, doesn't it?

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.660
I said, man, we putting
a man's body in there

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:43.860
next to his mother's grave.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:44.750
Yeah, it gets to me.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:46.930
And I said, let me tell
you something, young man.

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:49.140
The minute it doesn't
matter to you any more,

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:50.770
you need to leave the movement.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:52.370
It matters to me.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:54.660
Every one of these deaths.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:57.510
How much do you feel faith is

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:59.820
baked into the politics of America though?

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:03.060
Because it's very distinct
when you're looking in

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:04.740
that it's so linked.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:08.840
And you can see people on
different political sides

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:10.130
defending their views

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:13.410
or using sometimes Christianity
in particular to say,

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:14.830
this is God's will.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:16.820
Do you think that faith
is being weaponized

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:21.300
in American politics in a
way that is just unhealthy?

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:23.840
I think that it is
definitely being weaponized

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.040
in a way that's unhealthy,

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.070
because I think that we've used faith

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:33.070
as a way of making people other than us,

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:36.010
or that others then believe,

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:38.100
wicked, and therefore,

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:39.690
if you can make them wicked

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:44.690
and make them some kind of
anti what God would want,

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:48.160
that it justifies treating them unequally

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.160
and treating them unfairly,

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.330
which is why I think people
of faith have to say,

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:55.070
I'm motivated by my faith,

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:57.300
but I want to protect people's rights

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.050
that may not even have a faith.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:02.560
I remember when I came
out for LGBTQ rights,

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.340
many, many years ago,

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:07.590
and I even supported gay marriage,

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:10.930
some of the clergymen that I preached for

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.130
in their churches as a guest minister

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:14.220
said to me, Reverend Al,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.110
I don't know if we could have
you in the church this year

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.030
because a lot of our parishioners

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:20.150
are not with you on
that same-sex marriage.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:22.490
And I said, well, okay,
then I'm not coming.

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.460
I think when there is just
a blurring of the lines

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.300
as there still seems to
be in America at times

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:27.580
with faith and with politics,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:30.700
I mean, it can be that people
do not see it as the law.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.850
They see it as imposing
people's views on them.

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:35.550
And I wonder what you
think would come first,

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:39.850
an atheist president of
America or a female president?

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:44.850
Well, I probably would
say a female president.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:47.960
But I don't rule it out.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:52.000
I mean, when I got started as
a youngster in the movement,

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.920
I never thought I'd see a black president.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:58.140
So it would not shock me
that either would come,

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:00.660
but my guess would be a female president.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.410
We already now have a
female vice president.

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.410
Indeed. Well, it's still not happened.

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:07.620
But I suppose whatever
political side you're on,

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.460
the candidate has to believe in God

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:11.360
and has to prove it in some way,

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:14.220
which is striking regardless
of how you come at it.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:17.330
Just talking about the phrase
"righteous troublemaker,"

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:19.090
and I was thinking about what happened

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:20.893
just over a year ago on the Capitol.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:26.900
Those rioters would
probably describe themselves

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.620
in a similar way as
righteous troublemakers.

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:31.680
They believe in what they are doing.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.790
They believe in what they're marching for.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:35.850
And I suppose what would you say

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:38.330
being someone who's
protested your whole life

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:41.560
and being so divided
from those with a view

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:42.970
who have of what they're doing

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.370
being just as worthy as what you're doing?

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:49.300
I would say to them that
what will make the difference

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.550
of whether what you're doing
is righteous or unrighteous

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:54.340
is the end goal.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:55.990
What are you trying to do?

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:59.090
They were there that day, January 6th,

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.780
to stop the certification of an election

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:05.570
that the American people
had chosen a president.

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:07.940
If you're going to have a righteous cause,

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:10.570
the end, the goal must be righteous,

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:13.970
and your behavior to achieve
the goal must be righteous.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:17.540
Neither was the case in the
January 6th insurrection.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:20.770
Because some say what
happened on the Capitol

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:22.520
was the beginning of something,

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:23.820
not the end of something,

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:28.660
and 75 million people voted
for Trump in the last election.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:32.840
And I just wondered from your
side of things, as it were,

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:35.140
how do you see this playing out

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:37.510
if the sides just can't
talk to each other?

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:39.900
Because you've built movements.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:40.870
I think that

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:44.890
I agree with those that say January 6th

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:47.670
was the beginning of something.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:49.230
They are not going anywhere.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:53.240
They have gone into states,
now changing voting laws.

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.270
They have built an infrastructure

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:58.770
around this big lie that Trump really won,

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.930
and that they want to in some way

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:04.760
control how votes are counted
and how votes are done.

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:08.100
I think we're in for a long season

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:12.670
of confronting voting
rights and different sides

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:15.280
opposing different sides
on these questions.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.670
And I don't know that
we are going to be able

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:22.380
to get to this dialogue of
healing for a minute now.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:24.650
I think this is going to play itself out.

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:28.270
I think the election of Joe Biden

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:30.570
and the four years of Donald Trump

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:32.350
has divided the United States

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:35.100
more than I've seen it in my lifetime.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.240
Looking at movements then,

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:38.640
and looking at how things

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:41.460
can hopefully get better or improve.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.500
Of course, as I say,
you've been protesting,

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:46.220
you've been on the streets
for a long, long time.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.350
And you've seen things that
you couldn't have imagined,

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:50.910
like a black president,
like you just said.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:53.550
But I was just thinking about
tactics and changing approach.

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:55.940
Of course, many hailed the
Black Lives Movement protests

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:59.030
because they didn't erupt in violence.

00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:01.120
They didn't erupt in riots,

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:04.350
although there have also
been divisions within that.

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:07.720
Some factions calling for
the defunding of the police.

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:09.690
Just a word on that.

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:11.260
Where are you on that?

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:13.400
And where do you think
the movement is on that?

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:14.920
Because there is division.

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:17.820
I think as any movement,
there's always going to be

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:20.620
disagreements within the movement.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.480
I grew up in the north.

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:24.750
The year Martin Luther
King was killed, I was 13.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:28.620
I was already youth director
of his chapter in New York.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.560
There are people that
disagreed with Dr. King.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.540
They're saying that they
believed in self-defense,

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:33.600
not non-violence.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.350
They believed in...

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:37.340
Then you had those on the other side

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:39.470
that had condemned Dr. King

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:41.480
for coming out against the war in Vietnam,

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:43.020
saying that we should be

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.290
for whatever President Johnson wanted

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:47.930
because he was a good
civil rights president.

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:50.260
So, these disagreements exist.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:53.120
I think that we need to reform the police,

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:57.000
but we need to keep a measure
of policing in our communities

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:58.260
because we have high crimes.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:02.440
You've got as much a
problem with gun violence

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:05.490
as you have a problem
of dealing with police

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:09.860
that are over the line and
do things that are wrong

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:12.990
and that in many cases are criminal.

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:16.020
Also, I think that it is restructuring

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:19.700
how you put the funds and
how you fund policing.

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:21.080
But you must have policing.

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:23.410
Three weeks after I did the eulogy

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:25.000
for George Floyd's funeral,

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.980
I did the eulogy of a one-year-old child

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:30.370
that was killed in a crossfire

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.320
of gangs fighting in Brooklyn.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:33.780
So we need both.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:36.850
We need good policing, funded properly,

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:39.760
that deal with things like
mental health and gun control.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:42.130
And there's also of
course this push and hope

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:46.170
that the police themselves
will report bad cops,

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:48.350
will also come forward

00:17:48.350 --> 00:17:50.720
and have that culture change within there,

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:53.460
as well as the training and the policing

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:55.220
that they've got to do
that you talk about.

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:56.670
I'm just thinking about people

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:58.950
who may be thinking of themselves

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:01.750
in the way that you've described,
a righteous troublemaker,

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:03.300
talking about making good trouble,

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:05.650
as you said right at the
beginning of our conversation.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.790
And if you think back to your younger self

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:13.110
and how you've changed as a
protestor and your tactics,

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.550
what is your biggest regret,

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:17.530
and what's the lesson you've learned?

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:18.830
What I've learned is

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:22.440
that you should not shoot from the hip.

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:24.080
We often say tit-for-tat.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:26.430
Report or somebody say something,

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:30.190
I'm almost at the beginning
of their last word,

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:31.610
answering them,

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:35.690
be more thoughtful to
make sure whatever you say

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:39.090
and whatever you do and
whatever tactics you use

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:43.400
is toward achieving the
end goal that you profess.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:45.660
Because people are depending on you.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:48.560
And they expect you to be thoughtful,

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.450
not just theatrical.

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:51.480
One of the controversies

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:54.640
that is still brought up by
your critics is your response

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:58.200
to the 1991 Crown Heights
riots in New York.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:18:59.220
To remind our viewers,

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:01.410
these took place after a
seven-year-old black boy

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.010
was killed in a motorcade

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:05.130
by a motorcade transporting a rabbi.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:06.930
Violence broke out after that death

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:08.860
and a Jewish student was killed.

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:11.740
Your critics say you never
called on the rioters

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:14.820
to stop their
anti-Semitic-inspired violence.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:16.130
Is that fair?

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.250
That's totally erroneous.

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:21.650
Because as the state of
New York did a study,

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:23.670
I was not even in Crown Heights

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:26.950
when the young rabbi student was killed.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:28.220
I was called by the family

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:30.680
of the seven-year-old kid the next day.

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:35.330
But many in the fringe or
right wing try and distort

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.960
that I somehow should've called for them

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:40.670
not to do what they did that night,

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:42.610
when I was living in New Jersey.

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:45.490
But these are the kinds of
things that you have to deal with

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:46.530
and you keep going.

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:49.696
That is not only unfair. It's untrue.

00:19:49.696 --> 00:19:51.330
That's why I wanted to ask you about it.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:54.050
I mean, you did mark the 20th
anniversary of the incident.

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:55.960
You wrote an opinion piece

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.710
in the New York Daily News and you said,

00:19:57.710 --> 00:20:00.690
our language and tone
sometimes exacerbated tensions

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:03.240
and played to the extremists.

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:04.484
And actually there was a response

00:20:04.484 --> 00:20:08.390
from the young man's brother,
who responded saying,

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:11.920
he felt your recollections
were sanitized and distorted.

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:15.200
So there's obviously still
a lot of hurt and pain.

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:19.760
But again, we're talking 30
what? How many years ago?

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:21.860
All you have to do is
go to the state report,

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:24.420
who, by the way, criticized David Dinkins,

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:25.930
the mayor then, and others,

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:28.500
but said Sharpton was not there.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:31.280
Now I said that when I came in

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:32.760
that maybe I could have,

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:35.960
as I did the eulogy for the
seven-year-old kid's funeral,

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.980
said more things that would have been

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:39.813
that I would just,

00:20:39.813 --> 00:20:41.470
they would say differently.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:44.673
But to say that I incited
or excused violence,

00:20:44.673 --> 00:20:46.380
it's just not true.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.200
And even in his brother's statement,

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:51.250
saying sanitize or whatever,
as a matter of opinion,

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:54.090
he could not say that I incited violence.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:56.530
I suppose it's just when
you are looking back

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:58.780
at tactics and how protests happen

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:01.310
and the leadership I
suppose that you've had,

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:03.170
it's about also learning from that,

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:04.660
which is what I wanted to ask you about,

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:05.740
because you'll be well aware

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:08.170
of these criticisms over the years.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:11.340
Right, I think that what I've
learned and what I share,

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:12.850
just keep your eye on the prize.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:15.270
What is it that you're going for?

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:16.103
Don't react to the crowd.

00:21:16.103 --> 00:21:18.894
Don't play to the -

00:21:18.894 --> 00:21:21.810
Mrs. Martin Luther King, Coretta
Scott King used to tell me,

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:23.800
don't play to the cheap seats

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:25.860
to get the roaring of the crowd.

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:28.430
She said, think about what
you're trying to achieve

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:29.680
and speak to that.

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:31.390
And that's what I've had to learn.

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:34.070
I just wanted to ask about
the next generation of leaders

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:36.240
that you have in your sights,

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:37.930
particularly around racial equality.

00:21:37.930 --> 00:21:41.650
Are there some that you are
particularly keeping an eye on?

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:44.950
I have many in my National Action Network

00:21:44.950 --> 00:21:47.130
and some beyond that I'm looking at.

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:48.290
I won't call names

00:21:48.290 --> 00:21:50.590
but there are some that
are very impressive.

00:21:50.590 --> 00:21:52.750
And I tell them that they've got to learn

00:21:52.750 --> 00:21:55.180
in their generation what works

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:56.350
and they're going to be tested

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:58.090
and you're going to be criticized.

00:21:58.090 --> 00:22:01.520
And being a troublemaker,
you're going to have trouble,

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.470
because the fact that you're questioning,

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:05.870
speaking truth to power,

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:07.640
power isn't going to answer you.

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:11.140
So make sure you are absolutely committed

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:13.020
and want to be that and do that.

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:14.410
But keep your eye on the prize.

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:16.320
Don't make my mistakes.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:19.670
You can study people like me and others,

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:22.520
but study also where we fell short

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:24.000
and correct them as we go.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.540
And also health I imagine.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:27.800
You have an amazing regime.

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:30.190
You can keep going for longer I suppose.

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:31.290
If you don't mind me mentioning,

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:32.860
you've lost an enormous amount of weight

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:35.820
and you post about drinking
your kale juice every day

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:36.750
and you look great.

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:41.230
I've lost 176 pounds
over the last four years.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:42.860
I am a vegetarian.

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:44.280
Eat one meal a day.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:47.160
Work out 35 minutes every morning.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:50.870
And I'm in better shape
now at 67 than I was at 37.

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:52.587
Well, long may it continue.

00:22:52.587 --> 00:22:55.260
And do you work out
listening to James Brown?

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:57.010
Of course, a few gym grooves?
A lot of times I do.

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:57.930
He was like my dad.

00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:00.210
First time I went to London,
James Brown brought me.

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:02.110
He played at the Hammersmith Odeon.

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:04.680
And I worked with James Brown.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.750
He brought me to London and I listened.

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:08.990
A lot of his music is up-tempo.

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:10.200
So it helps me when I'm on the elliptical

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.180
or lifting weights.

00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:13.710
That's what you're doing it to.

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:14.800
Reverend Al Sharpton,

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:17.260
thank you very much indeed for your time,

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.480
your recollections, and
some of your lessons.

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:21.080
Thank you. God bless.

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:22.920
Thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:24.200
Until we meet again,

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:25.910
mask up, stay safe,

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:26.743
and goodbye.

